<!doctype html>
<html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8">
<title>Galaxy 40: forty categorical colors</title>
<style>body{font:16px/1.5 system-ui,sans-serif;max-width:960px;margin:2rem auto;padding:1rem;color:#1d2c42}img{width:100%;height:auto}code{background:#edf0f5;padding:1px 4px}</style>
</head><body>
<h1>Galaxy 40: 40-category scientific plots</h1>
<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Compare two qualitative 40-color candidates. These
colors represent category identities, not numeric magnitude. The example
is a <em>synthetic cluster embedding</em>, not an actual UMAP analysis.</p>
<!--begin.rcode setup, echo=FALSE
knitr::opts_chunk$set(fig.width=10,fig.height=4.4,dpi=120)
library(otherworldspalettes)
set.seed(40)
n <- 40L
theta <- seq_len(n) * pi * (3-sqrt(5))
radius <- sqrt(seq_len(n))*0.90
centers <- cbind(radius*cos(theta),radius*sin(theta))
group <- factor(rep(seq_len(n), each=32),levels=seq_len(n))
x <- rep(centers[,1],each=32)+rnorm(length(group),sd=.32)
y <- rep(centers[,2],each=32)+rnorm(length(group),sd=.32)
end.rcode-->

<h2>The same clusters, two different assignments</h2>
<!--begin.rcode clusters, echo=TRUE, fig.width=10, fig.height=5
op <- par(mfrow=c(1,2),mar=c(3,3,3,1))
for (nm in c("galaxy40","galaxy40_max")) {
  palette <- otherworlds_palette(n,nm)
  plot(x,y,pch=19,cex=.55,col=unname(palette)[as.integer(group)],
       main=nm,xlab="Embedding 1",ylab="Embedding 2")
  text(centers[,1],centers[,2],seq_len(n),cex=.58,font=2,col="#182437")
}
par(op)
end.rcode-->

<h2>Heatmap annotation strips</h2>
<p>Category strips can be more challenging than large cluster regions because
the marks are narrow. Labels, legends, and grouping conventions are vital.</p>
<!--begin.rcode strips, echo=TRUE, fig.width=10, fig.height=2.4
op <- par(mfrow=c(2,1),mar=c(2.4,3,2,1))
for(nm in c("galaxy40","galaxy40_max")) {
  barplot(rep(1,n),col=otherworlds_palette(n,nm),border=NA,
          space=0,axes=FALSE,main=nm)
}
par(op)
end.rcode-->

<p>Use stable factor levels across figures. Preserve \`cosmic\` color assignments
with <code>galaxy40</code>; choose <code>galaxy40_max</code> only if reassignment
of existing groups is acceptable. Neither option is guaranteed distinguishable
to every viewer. For a scientific publication, use direct labeling or
additional visual channels and review the figure in grayscale.</p>
</body></html>
